<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maba Diakhou Ba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1809">1809</a>–<a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Maba Jaxu Ba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maba Diakhou Ba was a Muslim cleric and leader of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegambia">Senegambia</a> region in the 1860s. From his base in Rip he conquered much of the Kingdom of Saloum and spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a>, resisting both traditional rulers and the French. He invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Kingdom of Sine</a>, but was defeated and killed at the battle of Fandane-Thiouthioune in 1867.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maba was born in 1809 in the region of Rip, also called Badibu, into a family descended from the Fulani Denianke dynasty; his grandfather had come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/futa-toro">Futa Toro</a>, and the family were teachers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>. He studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayor">Cayor</a> and taught in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolof-empire">Jolof</a>, his mother's homeland, where he married into the royal family. In 1846 he probably met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-hadj-umar-tall">El Hadj Umar Tall</a>, who named him the Tijani leader for the region, and around 1850 he returned to Rip, spending ten years teaching near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaolack">Kaolack</a> while the French under Governor Faidherbe waged a scorched-earth campaign against resistance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegambia">Senegambia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As court marabout to the weak king of Rip, Maba stockpiled weapons, and when the ceddo warriors attacked the marabouts his students defeated them. By 1861 he controlled all of Rip, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> soon took most of Saloum and part of Niumi; he founded Nioro as his capital. He enforced his reading of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> by burning and enslaving villages that resisted, and he was a prominent slave trader. In May 1864 he gave refuge to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lat-dior">Lat Dior</a>, the deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damel">Damel</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayor">Cayor</a>, and converted him, and later the Buur of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolof-empire">Jolof</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alboury-ndiaye">Alboury Ndiaye</a>, a turning point for Islam among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a>.</p><p>With up to 11,000 fighting men, he was recognised by the French in October 1864 as Almamy of Baddibu and Saloum. When Governor Émile Pinet-Laprade marched on Saloum with 1,600 regulars, 2,000 cavalry and 4,000 volunteers and foot soldiers, Maba's forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lat-dior">Lat Dior</a> drove him back at the Battle of Pathé Badiane outside Nioro. His movement also sought to abolish the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> system of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> states.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Maba invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Sine</a>, ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> king Kumba Ndoffene Diouf, burned its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diakhao">Diakhao</a>, and on 20 April 1867 killed the French captain Le Creurer at Thiofack. On 18 July 1867, at Fandane-Thiouthioune, a rainstorm made his men's guns useless, and he was killed in the rout. Sine's resistance kept much of Serer country outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> into the 20th century. The Tijani centres of Tivaouane and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaolack">Kaolack</a> both arose from his short-lived state, and his tomb at Fandène is a place of pilgrimage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maba Diakhou Bâ” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1860s the jihadist <b>Maba Diakhou Ba</b> conquered much of it, spreading Islam.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-saloum">Kingdom of Saloum</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1867 its army defeated and killed the jihadist <b>Maba Diakhou Ba</b> at Fandane-Thiouthioune.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-sine">Kingdom of Sine</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nioro du Rip Department, in what is now <a href="/countries/senegal">Senegal</a>. Died in Somb, in what is now <a href="/countries/senegal">Senegal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.74,-15.77&amp;year=1846" aria-label="Nioro du Rip Department on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Maba Diakhou Ba"><image href="/maps/globes/g33000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6170 3836h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6170 3836h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.74,-15.77&amp;year=1846">What was here in 1846? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
